# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_webhook_signature_robust.py" "WEBHOOK SIGNATURE ROBUSTNESS GATE: PASS" "webhook signature-verify robustness (a forged non-ASCII X-Hub-Signature-256 must REJECT cleanly, never crash the verify path)" "webhook signature-verify robustness (audit:security-auth 2026-06-20): verify_signature ended in hmac.compare_digest(expected_STR, sig_header_STR). CPython's compare_digest, given two str, requires BOTH to be ASCII-only and RAISES 'TypeError: comparing strings with non-ASCII characters is not supported' the instant the second operand carries any non-ASCII char. The X-Hub-Signature-256 header is FULLY attacker-controlled on the PUBLIC webhook endpoint, so a forged delivery with 'sha256=<64 non-ASCII chars>' (right prefix, right length) made the security-critical compare THROW instead of returning False -- the TypeError unwound out of verify_signature and do_POST, so the forgery NEVER reached the clean 401 default-deny path: the handler logged a traceback and abruptly dropped the connection (a 500-class outcome an unauthenticated attacker can spray to flood logs / probe the verify path). A control an unauthenticated caller can crash must fail CLOSED. FIX: compare on BYTES -- expected = b'sha256='+digest.encode('ascii'); sig_header.encode('utf-8','ignore') -- so a non-ASCII / odd header is a plain digest MISMATCH (False), in constant time, never a raise (encode errors='ignore' can only DROP bytes, shortening it -> still a guaranteed mismatch; the cheap structural sha256= prefix pre-screen on the str carries no secret in its timing). This gate drives the REAL verify_signature (re-exported as server.verify_signature) over: a VALID signature (still True -- valid-auth behaviour UNCHANGED), the existing default-deny set (missing/empty/wrong-prefix/wrong-bytes/uppercase-hex all still False, unchanged), and the DEFECT class -- five non-ASCII attacker headers (64 'e-acute', a single trailing non-ASCII on an otherwise-valid sig, emoji, full-width unicode digits, non-ASCII inside the prefix) each must return False with NO raise. Plus: empty-secret dev mode still accepts (unchanged), and the reject path never surfaces the webhook secret. PURE+OFFLINE (no DB, no network) -- a stateless HMAC compare; valid-signature verification is byte-for-byte unchanged." "webhook signature-verify robustness: a forged X-Hub-Signature-256 carrying non-ASCII bytes (attacker-controlled on the public endpoint) no longer crashes hmac.compare_digest with a TypeError -- the verify path now fails CLOSED (returns False, reaches the clean 401), comparing on bytes in constant time; valid-signature verification unchanged, secret never logged. Gate 132." 18
